  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-40409352" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2024 Sudan Emergency WASH Response: Addressing urgent humanitarian WASH needs in Kassala and Gedaref States</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">According to the INFORM Risk Index, Sudan ranks 8th globally in terms of humanitarian crises, with a score of 7.3, indicating a &apos;very high&apos; level of risk. This index assesses various factors such as the impact of the crisis, the conditions of affected populations, and the complexity of the conflict, predicting a worsening trend in the coming months. A record number of people, approximately one-third of the population, is already facing hunger in Sudan before the current conflict erupted. With GAM prevalence of 13.6 per cent Sudan had some of the highest levels of malnutrition in the world. The highest prevalence of food insecurity is expected in West Darfur, Kassala, West Kordofan, Blue Nile, Red Sea and North Darfur. WASH (Water, Sanitation, and Hygiene) services have particularly deteriorated due to the lack of potable water, inadequate sanitation infrastructure, inflation, increased fuel costs, and the growing demands from the influx of internally displaced persons (IDPs). Women and children are especially vulnerable due to entrenched social norms and limited access to essential services, which heightens their protection risks.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gedaref and Kassala States, Sudan</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The most vulnerable populations in Kassala and Gedaref States facing critical issues to accessing potable water, improving hygiene and sanitation infrastructures.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to address these critical issues by enhancing access to potable water, improving sanitation infrastructure, and supporting the most vulnerable populations in Kassala and Gedaref States. Through targeted interventions, the proposed project seeks to address the immediate WASH needs, while preparing SRCS to address upcoming anticipated climate risks that would exacerbate existing needs and mainstreaming protection, inclusion and community engagement activities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to address urgent humanitarian WASH needs focusing on the following results and activities including PGI, CEA and NSD:
Result 1: The most vulnerable displaced people have safe access to basic water, sanitation, and hygiene services
1. Water Supply 
2. Environmental health and sanitation
3. Hygiene promotion 
4. Vector control

Result 2: SRCS is prepared to respond to water-related hazards (flooding and epidemics) 
1. Preparedness and Anticipatory Action
2. EWASH in Health Facilities

PGI: Different people impacted by, displaced by and fleeing the crisis are safe from harm including violence, discrimination and exclusion, and their needs and rights are met;
1. Protection Against Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (PSEA) 
2. PGI Mainstreaming

CEA: To support the response to have a thorough understanding of community needs, priorities, and context, and integrate meaningful community participation, open and honest communication, and mechanisms to listen to and act on feedback throughout the response.
1. CEA mainstreaming 
2. feedback mechanism

NSD: To roll out SRCS business continuity plan for 2024-2025 so they are prepared to respond effectively to current crise.</narrative>
  </description>
